# Sample CODEOWNERS for a GenieRails customer repo (design section 18.1).
#
# Copy to .github/CODEOWNERS (GitHub) or adapt for your Git host, replace the
# @your-org/... teams with your own, and turn on "Require review from Code
# Owners" for main. The last matching pattern wins, so the agent rule comes
# after the env-wide governance rule.
#
#   Governance engineer: treatments, tag_treatments, tiers, group_map,
#     table_readers, row filters, top-level uc_tables, ungovern,
#     acknowledgements, and approving the governed-table set
#     -> envs/<env>/env.auto.tfvars, envs/<env>/generated/ (including
#        governed_tables.json), envs/<env>/ack.txt, envs/<env>/custom_masks/
#        (adopted custom masks, design 18.4.1)
#   Data citizen: curates an agent in the dev UI (including its tables),
#     captures it, promotes it -> envs/<env>/agents/<agent>.*
#
# An agent owns its table list, but governance approves every table an agent
# adds: make generate, make capture and make promote-to record each agent's
# tables in envs/<env>/generated/governed_tables.json (governance-owned), and
# make check fails while that record is stale. So an agent-only change that
# keeps its tables (make capture, then make promote-to) touches only agents/
# files and needs only its agent's owners; one that adds or removes a table
# also needs a governance owner. Access stays governance-only: SELECT comes
# only from table_readers in env.auto.tfvars. make promote-to prints which kind
# of files it changed. One team doing both can own both sets.

# GenieRails itself: product code and mappings change every env's generated
# output (an upgrade is a governance change).
/shared/                        @your-org/governance-engineers

# Every env file is governance-owned unless a later rule says otherwise:
# env.auto.tfvars, generated/, ack.txt, custom_masks/ (and the layer files of
# legacy envs).
# Each rule is anchored to the repo root and names a supported cloud tree, so
# no other directory called envs/ matches.
/aws/envs/                      @your-org/governance-engineers
/azure/envs/                    @your-org/governance-engineers
/aws/envs/*/ack.txt             @your-org/governance-engineers
/azure/envs/*/ack.txt           @your-org/governance-engineers
# Detached-agent tombstones live in ack.txt; agents/ edits alone cannot revive one.

# Agents: one settings file and one captured config per agent, in every env.
# Lifecycle revival additionally requires a governance-owned ack.txt change.
/aws/envs/*/agents/             @your-org/data-citizens
/azure/envs/*/agents/           @your-org/data-citizens
# Per-agent owners (optional): name each agent's team, e.g.
# /aws/envs/*/agents/Sales.*    @your-org/sales-analysts
# /azure/envs/*/agents/Sales.*  @your-org/sales-analysts

# Pipelines and repo settings.
/.github/                       @your-org/platform
/aws/.github/                   @your-org/platform
/azure/.github/                 @your-org/platform
/aws/azure-pipelines/           @your-org/platform
/azure/azure-pipelines/         @your-org/platform
